<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Yemen Eyalet</h1><p class="st-meta">1538 CE – 1636 CE · capital Sanaa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Ottoman province of Yemen, conquered to fight the Portuguese and lost a century later to the Zaydi imams.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1538 an <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> fleet under Hadim Suleiman Pasha, on its way to fight the Portuguese in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> pushed into the highlands, taking Sanaa in 1547 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ozdemir-pasha">Ozdemir Pasha</a>, and organised <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a> as a province.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ottoman governors relied on garrisons and allied tribes and faced constant resistance from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zaydi-imamate-of-yemen">Zaydi Imamate of Yemen</a>. A great revolt under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutahhar-ibn-sharaf-al-din">al-Mutahhar</a> in 1567 almost expelled them, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koca-sinan-pasha">Sinan Pasha</a> reconquered the country in 1569-1570. The province guarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> approach to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> trade through <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mohu">Mocha</a> began to grow in this period.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1597 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasim-ibn-muhammad">al-Qasim ibn Muhammad</a> began a new Zaydi revolt. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muayyad-muhammad">al-Mu'ayyad Muhammad</a> continued it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> left Sanaa and evacuated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a> in 1636, giving way to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qasimid-state">Qasimid State</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yemen-eyalet.jpg" alt="Yemen Eyalet" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Orwellianist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Ottoman_Empire_(1844%E2%80%931922).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1538 CE – 1636 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sanaa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ozdemir Pasha, Sinan Pasha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ottoman Turkish, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ottoman Yemen (1538-1636)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1587&amp;focus=yemen-eyalet" aria-label="Yemen Eyalet on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5866 1682 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yemen Eyalet lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6186 1938L5709 970M6186 1938L6207 1863"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M6207 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6186 1938h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6186 1938h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1587&amp;focus=yemen-eyalet">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

